.SH NAME
nysos \- Multi\-pane scripted and interactive terminal demonstrations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nysos
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run named, interactive shell panes alongside a manually advanced command
queue.
Configure layouts, colors, shells, and commands in a TOML demo file.
Loading a demo never automatically executes its queued commands.
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-demo\fR
Load the built\-in six\-cue demo of independent pane titles and themes
.TP
\fB\-\-debug\-keys\fR
Show the last key event and editor mode for shortcut troubleshooting
.TP
\fB\-\-terminal\-keys\fR \fI<PROFILE>\fR
Terminal key mappings: auto detects Ghostty from TERM_PROGRAM or TERM
.sp
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto
.IP \(bu 2
standard
.IP \(bu 2
ghostty
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-prefix\fR \fI<KEY>\fR
Override the demo control prefix (default: ctrl\-g; avoids default tmux
prefix)
.sp
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
ctrl\-a
.IP \(bu 2
ctrl\-b
.IP \(bu 2
ctrl\-g
.IP \(bu 2
f12
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-version\-full\fR
Print full version, Git metadata, compiler, target, and build profile
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Load a demo TOML file. No file is loaded automatically, including demo.toml.
Relative paths resolve from the launch directory.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
Validate TOML syntax, pane count, IDs, titles, weights, and command targets
without spawning shells. Use \-\-demo to check the built\-in demo; with
neither \-\-config nor \-\-demo, checks the empty interactive configuration.
Does not verify shell paths, working directories, or command syntax.
.TP
\fB\-\-init\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Write the built\-in six\-cue demo using /bin/sh and exit. The destination must
not exist and its parent directory must already exist. Cannot be combined with
\-\-config or \-\-check.
.TP
\fB\-\-add\-to\-path\fR [\fI<SHELL>\fR]
Add the running binary\*(Aqs directory to PATH for bash, zsh, or both
(default: all). Updates marked blocks in user startup files, preserves other
content, and backs up existing files. Use \-\-bin\-dir to select a stable
installation directory. Does not copy the executable or alter the current
shell.
.sp
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
bash
.IP \(bu 2
zsh
.IP \(bu 2
all
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-bin\-dir\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
Directory containing nysos to add to PATH
.TP
\fB\-\-install\-completions\fR [\fI<SHELL>\fR]
Install bash, zsh, or both completion scripts (default: all) under
$XDG_DATA_HOME/nysos/completions, falling back to ~/.local/share. Updates user
startup files to load them; respects ZDOTDIR. Safe to repeat. Can be combined
with \-\-add\-to\-path. Open a new terminal afterward.
.sp
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
bash
.IP \(bu 2
zsh
.IP \(bu 2
all
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B nysos
Start two interactive shells using SHELL, or /bin/sh, with an empty cue list.
.TP
.B nysos --demo
Load the built-in six-cue demo showing independent pane titles and themes.
.TP
.B nysos \-\-init demo.toml
Create a starter file without overwriting an existing file.
.TP
.B nysos \-\-config demo.toml \-\-check
Validate configuration without spawning shells or executing commands.
.TP
.B nysos \-\-config demo.toml
Start a saved demonstration. Commands run only when manually advanced.
.TP
.B nysos \-\-add-to-path \-\-install-completions
Configure PATH and completions for both Bash and Zsh, then exit.
.SH SHELL SETUP
The --add-to-path and --install-completions options accept bash, zsh, or all;
omitting the value selects all. They can be combined with each other, but not
with --config, --check, or --init. Open a new terminal after installation.
.PP
PATH setup uses the running executable's directory. It does not copy the
binary.
Use --bin-dir DIR to select a stable directory containing nysos, especially
with
versioned Nix store paths or profile symlinks. PATH entries are not
duplicated.
.PP
Bash setup updates .bashrc and the first existing .bash_profile, .bash_login,
or .profile, creating .profile only if none exists. Zsh PATH setup updates
\&.zprofile and .zshrc under ZDOTDIR, or HOME when ZDOTDIR is unset.
Completions
are loaded only for interactive shells; Zsh initializes compinit if needed.
.PP
Scripts are written under XDG_DATA_HOME/nysos/completions, falling back to
HOME/.local/share/nysos/completions: nysos.bash and _nysos. Rerun completion
installation after upgrading the binary.
.PP
Startup blocks are marked with nysos comments and updated in place. Existing
files get a .nysos.bak backup, which is never overwritten. Symlink targets are
updated while links are preserved. Each file replacement is atomic; the
overall
multi-file installation is not. Output reports updated and unchanged files.
Remove the marked blocks and generated scripts to undo shell setup.
.SH INTERACTIVE CONTROLS
Press Ctrl-G (or the prefix selected with --prefix), release, then one of the
following keys. Supported prefixes are ctrl-g, ctrl-a, ctrl-b, and f12.
.TP
.B n, Enter
Send the next command to its target pane and advance.
.TP
.B s
Skip the next command.
.TP
.B e
Edit the current queue item. Applying rewinds that item without executing it.
.TP
.B o
Open the full demo editor to change the title, add or reorder cues, or edit
panes. Ctrl-L loads a preview; Ctrl-S saves and
applies; Ctrl-G applies without saving; Esc cancels. F2, F3, and F4 are
aliases.
Applying a full demo resets queue progress. Matching shell sessions are
reused.
.TP
.B a
Add and focus an ad hoc shell pane, up to 16 panes total.
.TP
.B Tab, Right / Shift-Tab, Left
Focus the next / previous pane. Keys 1 through 9 select a pane directly.
.TP
.B 0, c
Show and focus the cue list, or toggle its visibility, respectively.
.TP
.B l, h
Cycle the layout or toggle the header, respectively.
.TP
.B x
Restart the focused shell, ending its current session.
.TP
.B ?, q
Show help or quit, respectively. Esc closes help.
.PP
In the cue list, Up/Down selects, Enter sends the selected cue, and e edits
it.
Plain p previews the selected cue\(aqs next pending command over each pane.
Panes without pending commands say so. Up/Down scrolls long preview text; Esc
closes all previews. Previewing executes nothing and leaves progress
unchanged.
Plain t types the first remaining command per target pane without sending
Enter,
then focuses the first target pane. Edit and submit each line in its shell.
Start
at empty shell prompts; input is inserted at the cursor. Control characters
(including tabs) are rejected. Progress is unchanged; skip manually submitted
commands or select a later cue to avoid sending them again.
Plain o opens the full demo editor to add cues or edit the demo title.
Home/End and Page Up/Down browse; the wheel scrolls the list. Click selects
without executing. Esc returns to the previous shell. Enter resumes the
current
partially sent cue or replays an earlier cue. Commands are sent in order
without
waiting for completion. Selection advances, staying on the last cue at the
end;
Enter on the completed last cue replays it. Ctrl-G n still sends one command.
.PP
Without the prefix, normal input and Ctrl-C go to the focused shell.
Alt-Left/Right rotates focus. In the ghostty key profile Alt-B/F also rotates
focus. Ghostty on macOS sends Alt-B/F for
Option-Left/Right; these also become focus shortcuts instead of shell word
navigation. Alt-Up/Down is forwarded to the shell. Click to focus; drag shared
borders to resize.
Grid row heights remain equal. Wheel events scroll history or reach
applications
requesting mouse input; Shift-wheel requests history when forwarded by the
host.
.PP
Cmd-click opens HTTP(S) links locally on macOS when the terminal forwards the
click. Alt-click is the portable fallback. Plain URLs spanning rows are not
joined.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Demo files use TOML. No file is automatically discovered. The top-level fields
are title (nonempty text, default "nysos demo"), prefix (ctrl-g by default),
header (boolean, default true), cue_list (boolean, default true),
cue_width (16 to 60 columns, default 26, capped at half the available width),
layout (columns, rows, or grid), panes, and
queues. Omitted panes use the two interactive defaults; omitted queues are
empty.
Use queues = [] for an interactive-only demo with custom panes.
.PP
Each pane table has id (unique identity), optional title (display label),
shell, args, optional cwd,
scheme (ocean, ember, forest, mono), and weight (1 to 1000). There must be 1
to 16
panes. The shell defaults to SHELL or /bin/sh; args defaults to an empty
array;
id defaults to shell; title defaults to id; scheme defaults to ocean; weight
defaults to 1.
.PP
Each queue table requires a nonempty name and one or more commands, with at
most one command per pane within that queue. Repeated targets are rejected.
The same pane may be used in another queue. Each queue has an
optional description. Each command requires pane (an existing pane ID) and
command (nonempty single-line input, with no NUL, CR, or LF).
Optional command title and scheme change the target pane when sent or typed,
without changing its ID or restarting its shell. Overrides persist until
changed;
preview and skip do not apply them. Titles must be nonempty without control
characters.
Saving preserves initial pane settings and cue overrides; full-demo apply
restores
initial appearance. Legacy pane name is an alias for id; do not specify both.
.PP
Paths are relative to the launch directory; configured paths do not expand
shell variables or tilde. The --check option validates structure and
references,
not executable paths, directories, command syntax, or service availability.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHELL
Default shell executable for panes that omit shell. The built-in and exported
six-cue example explicitly uses /bin/sh.
Falls back to /bin/sh when unavailable.
.TP
.B TERM, COLORTERM
Child PTYs receive xterm-256color and truecolor, respectively.
.TP
.B SSH_CONNECTION, SSH_TTY
If either is present, local macOS Command-key sampling is disabled.
.TP
.B HOME, ZDOTDIR, XDG_DATA_HOME
Control the user shell installation locations. HOME must be absolute.
A relative XDG_DATA_HOME is ignored; relative ZDOTDIR resolves from the launch
working directory.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B demo.toml
Initial save-path suggestion when no configuration file is selected. Not
loaded automatically.
.TP
.B docs/reference/configuration.md
Complete configuration reference in the source distribution.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including help, version, initialization, validation, or normal exit.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration, file, PTY, or runtime failure.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments.
.SH NOTES
Each advance sends one command plus Enter to the target pane's current
foreground
program. Wait for a shell prompt; there is no completion detection or
scheduler.
Changing a pane's id, shell, args, or cwd starts a replacement session.
Closing panes signals their shells; deliberately detached jobs may outlive
them.
.PP
macOS is the primary platform, Linux secondary. Native Windows interaction is
deferred; use WSL. An interactive terminal is required on stdin and stdout,
except for help, version, --init, --check, and shell setup options.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR zsh (1)
.PP
The source distribution contains user guides under docs/guide/.
.SH BUILD INFORMATION
The --version and --version-full switches report the package version, Git
commit,
exact tags, Git description, compiler, target, and build profile. The -V
switch
prints only the name and package version. Information is embedded at build
time;
Git is not required at runtime. Unavailable metadata is shown as unknown.
Nix builds preserve the flake revision but generally lack tag metadata.

.SH TMUX AND SSH
Ctrl-G avoids the default tmux Ctrl-B prefix. Enable tmux mouse forwarding
with
"tmux set-option -g mouse on". With --prefix ctrl-b under default tmux, press
Ctrl-B twice before each nysos command. nysos does not change tmux bindings.
.PP
Use ssh -t to allocate a remote terminal. Run nysos inside remote tmux to
retain
sessions across disconnects. Prefix-Tab works when Alt-arrow keys are
intercepted.
URL openers run on the remote host, not the SSH client. Native macOS
Command-key
sampling is disabled over SSH and inside tmux; use client terminal link
detection
for a client-side browser. Modal editor shortcuts are independent of the
prefix.

.SH TERMINAL KEY PROFILES
The --terminal-keys option and terminal_keys TOML field accept auto (default),
standard, or ghostty. Auto detects Ghostty from TERM_PROGRAM=ghostty or, when
TERM_PROGRAM is absent or tmux, TERM=xterm-ghostty. Other terminal identities
use standard mappings, preserving Alt-B/F for the shell. Both profiles support
standard Alt-Left/Right. Use an explicit profile when tmux or SSH masks the
client
terminal identity, or when terminal keybindings differ from their defaults.
Detection uses the launch environment; reconnecting tmux does not re-detect
it.
The resolved profile appears in the help title.
